    "verifier": {
      "passed": true,
      "blocking_issues": [
        {
          "check": "source_support",
          "severity": "warning",
          "detail": "E4 (Erasmus+ grants cover tuition) is cited as 'stated' tier but comes from a blog/media synthesis, not the policy's own text. The claim is plausible and well-supported elsewhere but the tier label is slightly loose \u2014 the policy text only says 'expand opportunities', not detail grant structures."
        },
        {
          "check": "source_support",
          "severity": "warning",
          "detail": "E17 (IFS study, 2011/2008) is correctly labelled projected but the rationale uses it to support a link between Erasmus+ and employability gains without noting the study predates Erasmus+ in its current form and measures general study-abroad effects, not Erasmus+ specifically. Not blocking but worth flagging."
        }
      ],
      "notes": "The verdict is well-constructed. Tiers are correctly applied throughout \u2014 the \u00a3570m cost is projected (government estimate), historical participation figures are measurable, and the IFS finding is kept projected. The direction of 'improves' is well-supported: multiple claims document Turing Scheme's inferiority in scope, funding, and reciprocity, and Erasmus+ clearly restores those dimensions relevant to O7 (skills, equity of access, vocational/school/adult coverage). The one-year review uncertainty is honestly flagged in biggest_unknown and constrains confidence to moderate, which is appropriate. No tier_integrity, lever_leakage, party_blind, false_balance, or magnitude_timing violations found. Moderate magnitude is justified given small absolute participant numbers relative to total student population and political sustainability risk."
    },
